WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.325
Multilingual language models must decide how much
to train on each language,

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:09.197
and the standard answer has been temperature-based
sampling.

00:00:09.277 --> 00:00:12.423
This paper from Google Research introduces UniMax,

00:00:12.503 --> 00:00:18.231
a simple sampling method that gives more uniform
coverage to high-resource languages while

00:00:18.311 --> 00:00:23.330
explicitly capping how many times any low-resource
language's data is repeated.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:28.170
Across many benchmarks and model scales, UniMax
beats temperature sampling,

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.557
and the gains persist as models grow.

00:00:30.637 --> 00:00:38.172
The authors also release a refreshed mC4 corpus of
twenty-nine trillion characters across one hundred seven languages,

00:00:38.252 --> 00:00:41.270
along with umT5 checkpoints trained with UniMax.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:45.252
So why does language sampling matter so much?

00:00:45.332 --> 00:00:48.934
Massively multilingual corpora are wildly imbalanced.

00:00:49.014 --> 00:00:52.616
In the mC4 dataset, English alone has about nine
point seven trillion characters,

00:00:52.696 --> 00:00:56.298
more than ninety-two thousand times the data
available for the lowest-resource language,

00:00:56.378 --> 00:00:59.980
Yoruba.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.662
If you train in proportion to the raw data,

00:01:03.742 --> 00:01:07.344
the tail languages barely register at all.

00:01:07.424 --> 00:01:11.026
Deciding how to balance them is an open and
expensive question,

00:01:11.106 --> 00:01:14.708
and the field's default answer, temperature-based
sampling,

00:01:14.788 --> 00:01:18.390
had never actually been evaluated systematically
across model scales.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:24.024
Temperature sampling reshapes the data distribution
using a single exponent, tau.

00:01:24.104 --> 00:01:25.407
But there is a catch.

00:01:25.487 --> 00:01:29.293
When you tune tau to give the head languages more
presence,

00:01:29.373 --> 00:01:31.598
you end up over-repeating the tail.

00:01:31.678 --> 00:01:36.076
At tau equals three point three three, with a
trillion-token budget,

00:01:36.156 --> 00:01:40.620
the lowest-resource languages get repeated more
than a hundred times.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:45.098
That much repetition causes overfitting that hurts
downstream tasks,

00:01:45.178 --> 00:01:48.325
it raises the risk of memorizing private content,

00:01:48.405 --> 00:01:51.223
and it wastes compute on duplicate examples,

00:01:51.303 --> 00:01:55.240
and every one of these harms only gets worse as
models scale.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.327
UniMax makes four contributions.

00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:03.113
First, the method itself,

00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:06.900
which allocates the training budget uniformly while
capping how many times any single language repeats.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.687
Second, an extensive ablation of sampling
strategies run across model scales,

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:14.473
the systematic study the field had been missing.

00:02:14.553 --> 00:02:18.260
Third,

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.047
a refreshed mC4 corpus of twenty-nine trillion
characters spanning one hundred seven languages.

00:02:22.127 --> 00:02:25.833
And fourth, released umT5 checkpoints trained with UniMax,

00:02:25.913 --> 00:02:29.620
so practitioners can build on this work directly.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.594
Here is how UniMax works.

00:02:33.674 --> 00:02:37.268
The name means uniform plus max.

00:02:37.348 --> 00:02:40.943
You start from a fixed character budget, C,

00:02:41.023 --> 00:02:44.617
and distribute it as uniformly as possible across
the languages,

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:48.291
processing them from lowest to highest resource.

00:02:48.371 --> 00:02:51.965
At each step,

00:02:52.045 --> 00:02:55.639
the algorithm checks whether the remaining
per-language budget can still be split evenly.

00:02:55.719 --> 00:02:59.313
If a language would exceed N epochs over its own
corpus, it is capped at N epochs,

00:02:59.393 --> 00:03:02.988
and the freed budget is redistributed uniformly
among the languages that remain.

00:03:03.068 --> 00:03:06.662
The result is more uniform coverage of the head languages,

00:03:06.742 --> 00:03:10.336
while no tail language is ever repeated more than N times.

00:03:10.416 --> 00:03:14.010
With the default of N equals one, nothing repeats at all.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:19.113
For evaluation, pretraining uses that refreshed mC4
corpus,

00:03:19.193 --> 00:03:23.916
twenty-nine trillion characters across one hundred
seven languages.

00:03:23.996 --> 00:03:28.719
The trained models are then tested on a broad suite
of tasks: TyDi QA GoldP,

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:33.521
WMT21 multilingual translation, XNLI, XQuAD, MLQA,
and PAWS-X.

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:38.324
Crucially,

00:03:38.404 --> 00:03:43.127
every sampling strategy is compared while sweeping
the model scale from Small all the way up to XXL,

00:03:43.207 --> 00:03:47.930
so the study can isolate whether the benefits
actually persist as the models get bigger.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:51.153
And the results are remarkably consistent.

00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:54.285
UniMax outperforms both temperature settings,

00:03:54.365 --> 00:03:58.044
tau equals one and tau equals three point three three,

00:03:58.124 --> 00:04:01.385
on average TyDi QA across all three model sizes,

00:04:01.465 --> 00:04:06.257
and it beats temperature sampling on WMT21
translation at every scale,

00:04:06.337 --> 00:04:09.459
with the majority of language pairs improving.

00:04:09.539 --> 00:04:14.401
On high-resource languages it beats tau equals
three point three three,

00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:18.368
and only trails tau equals one at the very largest scale.

00:04:18.448 --> 00:04:21.361
On low-resource languages it wins outright,

00:04:21.441 --> 00:04:28.530
even outperforming tau equals three point three
three on Swahili despite seeing fewer Swahili examples.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.535
What about that max-epoch parameter, N?

00:04:31.615 --> 00:04:33.338
Ablating N over one, five,

00:04:33.418 --> 00:04:38.124
and ten on Large models gives TyDi QA scores of
eighty-two point two,

00:04:38.204 --> 00:04:41.592
eighty-one point five, and eighty-one point eight.

00:04:41.672 --> 00:04:45.684
Disallowing repeats entirely, N equals one, comes
out best,

00:04:45.764 --> 00:04:47.556
though the effect is small.

00:04:47.636 --> 00:04:52.273
At a four-times-larger budget, UniMax scores
eighty-three point one,

00:04:52.353 --> 00:04:58.515
versus eighty-two point eight for temperature and
eighty-one point two for tau equals one.

00:04:58.595 --> 00:05:04.757
The loss curves tell the story: high-temperature
overfitting grows more severe with scale,

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:06.560
while UniMax stays stable.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.879
Let us put the headline numbers together.

00:05:09.959 --> 00:05:16.432
The refreshed mC4 has twenty-nine trillion
characters across one hundred seven languages,

00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:19.083
a thirty-five percent size increase.

00:05:19.163 --> 00:05:24.238
On TyDi QA at the larger budget, UniMax scores
eighty-three point one,

00:05:24.318 --> 00:05:26.005
ahead of both baselines.

00:05:26.085 --> 00:05:30.497
The umT5 XXL checkpoint beats the earlier mT5 XXL
on TyDi QA.

00:05:30.577 --> 00:05:34.326
And remember, at tau equals three point three three,

00:05:34.406 --> 00:05:40.290
the lowest-resource languages were being repeated
more than a hundred times over.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.100
The takeaway is simple.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.155
Capping per-language repeats, and spreading the
rest of the budget uniformly,

00:05:47.235 --> 00:05:50.832
beats temperature sampling for multilingual pretraining,

00:05:50.912 --> 00:05:53.524
and that advantage holds as models scale.

00:05:53.604 --> 00:05:58.710
UniMax is a drop-in, hyperparameter-light
replacement for temperature sampling,

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:03.700
and it ships with a refreshed corpus and umT5
checkpoints you can use today.
